WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:06.000
Was there almost a plan for a new actor to
play T'Challa after Chadwick Boseman's tragic death?

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.750
2020 saw the tragic passing of Chadwick Boseman.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:14.634
The T'Challa actor died from colon cancer
months before "Black Panther: Wakanda Forever"

00:00:14.634 --> 00:00:16.804
was set to begin production.

00:00:16.865 --> 00:00:22.110
In light of the tragedy, Marvel Studios postponed
the highly-anticipated sequel, giving the

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:27.189
cast and crew time to grieve over the late
actor and decide on next steps.

00:00:27.189 --> 00:00:31.929
Director Ryan Coogler and Marvel Cinematic
Universe architect Kevin Feige ultimately

00:00:31.929 --> 00:00:36.870
decided not to recast the role of T'Challa,
a decision that led to mixed reception from

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:37.870
some fans.

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:43.927
"He was king and Black Panther to everyone."

00:00:43.927 --> 00:00:49.379
While some fans petitioned the studio to recast
the King of Wakanda, Marvel VP of Development

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:54.500
Nate Moore made it very clear: T'Challa will
not be seen on screen again out of respect

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.760
for Boseman.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:00.770
He revealed on the Ringer-Verse podcast in
November 2021, months after "Wakanda Forever"

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:02.090
had started production,

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:07.010
"When Chad passed, it was a real conversation
we had with [director Ryan] Coogler about,

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:08.100
'What do we do?'

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.120
and it was a fast conversation.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:14.640
It wasn't weeks, it was minutes of we had
to figure out how to move that franchise on

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.040
without that character.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:22.131
Because I think we all feel so much of T'Challa
in the MCU on the screen ... is tied to Chadwick's performance."

00:01:22.131 --> 00:01:27.450
The marketing for "Wakanda Forever" has directly
confirmed that T'Challa has passed in-universe,

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:32.329
though it remains to be seen how Coogler and
co-writer Joe Robert Cole tackle it.

00:01:32.329 --> 00:01:37.250
Scrapping away the original idea for the "Black
Panther" sequel may have been difficult, but

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:41.970
from the rave social media reactions "Wakanda
Forever" has received, it seems that Coogler

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:43.750
has delivered another winner.

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:48.060
But while Moore emphasized how quickly the
decision had to be made, Ryan Coogler has

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:51.650
confirmed that he considered recasting T'Challa
at one point.

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:56.470
The director recently appeared on Marvel Studios'
"Wakanda Forever: The Official Black Panther"

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:58.800
podcast to discuss the sequel.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:03.430
The nearly 40-minute long chat dived deep
into the world of Wakanda, how the Marvel

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:09.000
Cinematic Universe has evolved, and how Chadwick
Boseman's death influenced and changed the sequel.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.620
The conversation eventually led to the director
being asked to discuss the significance and

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.510
responsibility of a new actor taking on the
role of Black Panther.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:19.379
Coogler candidly replied,

00:02:19.379 --> 00:02:24.160
"You consider everything, when something like
that happens, it's like 'I don't think I can

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.560
come back and make another one, I don't think
I can do this.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:29.550
I don't think there should be another one.'

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.420
You go through all of the extremes."

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:35.670
Getting emotional, Coogler confirmed that
recasting was considered an option before

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:40.840
opening up about why the thought of recasting
dissipated, and it all boils to being honest

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:42.370
and seeking out the truth.

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:46.770
He continued by opening up about how his job
as a director is to spend hundreds of days

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:51.000
convincing his cast and crew to believe in
ideas that he finds truthful.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.920
"I have to believe in something enough to
go convince other professionals to believe

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.680
in it as well for an extended period of time."

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.659
Expanding on that sentiment, Coogler explained
how his responsibility is to "weed out" anything

00:03:02.659 --> 00:03:06.470
in the film or creative that screams, quote,
"nope" to him.

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:10.384
"I have [to have] a fighting chance of putting
something out in front of the audience, that

00:03:10.384 --> 00:03:14.909
the audience can believe in and not be taken
out of for two hours ... they have to believe

00:03:14.909 --> 00:03:17.599
in some kernel of truth that's happening on
the screen."

00:03:17.599 --> 00:03:22.000
In the pursuit of his truth, which shaped
"Wakanda Forever," Coogler was clear about

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.000
one thing:

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:27.000
"Chadwick's gone from the physical sense,
he wasn't walking through that door ... he

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.130
was the guy."

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:33.170
Coogler recognized that if he himself didn't
believe in the idea of recasting T'Challa,

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:34.939
there was no truth in his vision.

00:03:34.939 --> 00:03:40.581
"For somebody else to be him, for us in the
world that we created, we wouldn't have believed it.

00:03:40.581 --> 00:03:44.730
No matter how good the actor was, it would've
been lacking the necessary truth for us to

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:46.340
do a good job."

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:50.840
It sounds like the idea of seriously recasting
Boseman's iconic role wasn't a path that Coogler

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:52.879
was interested in going down.

00:03:52.879 --> 00:03:57.200
The writer-director had some more fond words
for the dearly departed actor, saying,

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:00.516
"Our truth was lost, which is a fact of life.

00:04:00.516 --> 00:04:02.939
It's the gift and the curse of life.

00:04:02.939 --> 00:04:04.896
Heroes, great men, die."

